# Question 6 — Cholesterol and Enzymes
Total: 12 marks

## 6(a) — 3 marks

Focus: Why reducing cholesterol may reduce coronary heart disease.

Linked sequence:
1. Less cholesterol accumulates in coronary arteries.
2. Atheroma or plaque formation is reduced.
3. Blood can continue flowing to cardiac muscle.
4. Cardiac cells receive oxygen for aerobic respiration.

Three valid linked points can earn full marks.

## 6(b) — 1 mark

Focus: Structural differences between peptides.

Accepted features include:
- Number of amino acids.
- Amino acid sequence.
- Types or proportions of amino acids.

Two appropriate features are required for the single mark.

## 6(c)(i) — 2 marks

Focus: Laboratory simulation of protein digestion.

- Simulates digestion in the human body.
- Releases proteins or their active components.
- Digestive enzymes break peptide bonds.

## 6(c)(ii) — 1 mark

- Different digestive enzymes act in different regions of the digestive system.
- Different enzymes may act on different protein substrates.

## 6(c)(iii) — 2 marks

Focus: Experimental conditions.

- 37°C approximates human body temperature and supports human enzyme activity.
- Two hours provides sufficient time for digestion or peptide production.

## 6(d) — 3 marks

Focus: Soybean peptides and cholesterol synthesis.

- Increasing peptide concentration increases inhibition of HMGCR.
- Peptides interact with the enzyme or compete with its substrate.
- Less cholesterol precursor is converted into cholesterol.

