# Question 7 — Mutations
Total: 14 marks

## 7(a)(i) — 2 marks

- Substitution replaces one nucleotide or base with another.
- Insertion and deletion alter the number of nucleotides in DNA.

## 7(a)(ii) — 4 marks

Focus: Effects of mutations on protein function.

Relevant points:
- Some substitutions produce no change in amino acid sequence.
- Some substitutions occur in non-coding DNA.
- A substitution may alter only one amino acid.
- An insertion or deletion may produce a frameshift.
- A frameshift can alter multiple downstream amino acids.
- Protein function may therefore be affected differently.

## 7(a)(iii) — 1 mark

Focus: Advantages of a model organism.

- A larger number of animals may be sampled.
- Variables may be easier to control.

A sample-size argument must make the appropriate comparison.

## 7(b)(i) — 2 marks

- Draw an appropriate line of best fit.
- Use the line to obtain a whole-number estimate within the accepted range: 285–1143.

The exact estimate depends on the plotted line.

## 7(b)(ii) — 3 marks

Focus: Mutations and lifespan across species.

Accept three relevant patterns:
- Mutations increase with age.
- Species differ in lifespan.
- Species differ in accumulated mutations.
- Mutation rates differ between species.
- Variation differs between species.

## 7(b)(iii) — 2 marks

Possible explanations:
- Cell division frequency differs.
- DNA repair processes differ.
- Exposure to mutagenic factors differs.
- DNA content or genetic sequences differ.

